  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Now, combine your new understanding of factoring with using the <term class="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term> to solve equations.</para>
          <para>Solve the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) by any method. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the solution to the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) and your explanation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x=\frac {5}{2}\) (or 2.5) and \(x=1\). Rewriting the expression in factored form gives \((2x − 5)(x − 1) = 0\). Using the zero product property: \(2x − 5 = 0\) or \(x − 1 = 0\), so \(x=\frac {5}{2}\) and \(x = 1\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note>
        <para>Building Character: Curiosity</para>
          <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_12_12_01.png" />
          </media>
        </figure>
            <para>People typically spend more time learning about things they are curious about and more readily remember the information. Curious people tend to be happier individuals.</para>
            <para>Think about your current sense of curiosity. Are the following statements are true for you?</para>
            <list>
          <item> When I didn’t know the answer to a question, I couldn’t rest until I figured it out. </item>
          <item> I explored a completely new idea or topic—just for the fun of it. </item>
        </list>
            <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
      </note>
    </section>
